<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Supporting Our Community – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Supporting Our Community</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Does the Government Support Communities?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create instructions for everyday activities. Interactive: Write a group text on various topics, collaboratively, to be shared in a digital format.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can create a pamphlet with clear instructions explaining how the government or local authorities support a community event (e.g., "The event begins at 8:30 a.m. You should arrive at the community center by 8:00 a.m. for the Panama Verde clean-up, which is supported by the local environmental agency. Then, check in at the registration booth.") Can collaboratively write a group text on community issues to share online (e.g., "The community clean-up event will be on Saturday at 10 a.m. Volunteers have been working hard to prepare for the event. Join us at the park!") Can review and revise group writing for clarity before digital publication (e.g., "I think the post should be checked for correct spelling and the message should be reviewed for clarity before it is shared online.")
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the writing focus for the lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in a "Community Support Brainstorm." The teacher displays images of local community events (e.g., park clean-up, food drive, health fair). Students, in pairs, list ways the government or local authorities might "support," "fund," or "organize" these events. They use vocabulary like "resources," "volunteers," "programs," "initiative."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing a simple instruction or a statement about community support using the passive voice. For example: "The park clean-up *is organized* by the municipality." or "Volunteers *have been working* hard to prepare the area." The teacher explicitly highlights how the passive voice emphasizes the action/recipient rather than the doer, and how modals like "should" are used for advice/necessity. The teacher also reads the sentences aloud, focusing on the stress pattern for content words (e.g., "Com-MU-ni-ty PRO-grams are FUN-ded by the GOV-ern-ment").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who or what usually does the action in a passive voice sentence?" (Focus on the 'by' phrase or absence)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'You *should contribute* to the clean-up,' what am I telling you to do?" (Give advice/recommendation)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Supporting Our Community". Students analyze authentic examples of written communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents examples of an informational pamphlet excerpt and a social media post related to community support. The pamphlet might have instructions for participating in a "Panama Verde" clean-up event, and the social media post could be a group text inviting volunteers for a community garden project. Both texts will contain explicit examples of passive voice, modals for necessity, present perfect continuous, and the target vocabulary (e.g., "The event *is supported* by the local environmental agency," "You *should register* online," "Volunteers *have been working* on the garden all week," "The community development *is promoted* by local initiatives.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Written Expression: Students will comprehend and analyze the structure and linguistic features of written instructions and collaborative digital messages related to community support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students work individually to read the texts and answer gist questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main purpose of the pamphlet? What about the social media post?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who is the target audience for each text?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Identify two sentences from the texts where the government's or local authorities' involvement is explained using the passive voice."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Find a sentence that uses a modal verb to tell people what they *should* or *must* do."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing. Students engage in controlled and semi-controlled writing practice to solidify grammar and vocabulary.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using passive voice (e.g., "Community programs *are funded* by the government"), modals for necessity (e.g., "Individuals *should contribute* to community projects"), and present perfect continuous (e.g., "Volunteers *have been working* to improve community services"), integrating key vocabulary like "government," "programs," "resources," "volunteers," "support," "fund," "improve," "participate," "initiative."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Sentence Transformation):</b> Students receive sentences in active voice related to government support (e.g., "The city council funds the community park." or "The local agency cleans up the river.") and rewrite them in the passive voice, ensuring correct tense and subject-verb agreement (e.g., "The community park *is funded* by the city council." "The river *is cleaned up* by the local agency."). They also complete sentences with appropriate modals of necessity. (e.g., "Everyone ____ (contribute) to local projects." -> "Everyone *should contribute* to local projects.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Paragraph Writing):</b> In small groups, students write short descriptive paragraphs (3-4 sentences) about how the government or a local initiative *has been* supporting an imagined community project (e.g., "a new shelter for underserved families" or "a youth development program"). They must use at least one sentence with the present perfect continuous and three target vocabulary words. Example: "The local government *has been providing* resources for the new community shelter. Volunteers *have been dedicating* their time to build it. This *initiative* will greatly *improve* the lives of *underserved* families."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction/Peer Check - Instruction Writing):</b> Students work in pairs to write 3-4 instructions for participating in a hypothetical community event (e.g., "How to get involved in the local volunteer brigade"). They must include at least one passive voice sentence and two sentences with modals of necessity. They then exchange their instructions with another pair for peer review, checking for grammatical accuracy, clarity, and use of target vocabulary. The teacher circulates, observing and providing feedback, paying attention to stress patterns if students read their work aloud.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Supporting Our Community". Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Writing Project – "Digital Community Outreach". In groups of 3-4, students will choose one of the following tasks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option A: Create a short informational pamphlet (digital format)</b> with clear instructions explaining how the government or local authorities support a specific community event (e.g., a "Public Health Awareness Day" or a "Local Farmers' Market"). The pamphlet should include 4-5 key instructions or pieces of information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option B: Write a group text / social media post collaboratively</b> to be shared in a digital format (e.g., Instagram, community forum) about a local community issue or event, focusing on how support *is provided* or *should be provided*. This post should be 4-6 sentences long.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups decide on their task (pamphlet or social media post) and brainstorm ideas for a community event or issue (e.g., "youth development programs," "environmental clean-up," "support for underserved areas").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students collaboratively draft their chosen written product, ensuring the integration of:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">At least two passive voice sentences (e.g., "Resources *are allocated* by the government for these *programs*.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">At least two sentences using modals of necessity (e.g., "Volunteers *should participate* actively in the *decision-making* process.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">At least one sentence with the present perfect continuous (e.g., "Local authorities *have been promoting* civic *involvement* throughout the year.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">A minimum of five target vocabulary words (e.g., "government," "programs," "resources," "services," "responsibilities," "volunteers," "projects," "community," "influence," "development," "impact," "initiative," "support," "fund," "improve," "promote," "contribute," "address," "participate," "engage," "provide," "develop," "clean up," "underserved," "meaningful," "positive," "crucial," "responsible," "diverse," "active," "impactful," "essential," "appropriate," "local," "supportive," "governmental," "public," "community-based," "involved," "collaborative," "high-risk").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups discuss and refine their writing, focusing on clarity, coherence, and accurate grammar. They consider pragmatic aspects like "How has the local government been encouraging more people to volunteer in our community?" or "Why has community involvement been important for everyone?" as they write.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One member from each group will read their final product aloud to another group to practice rhythm and fluency, and also to check for clarity.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer assessment using a checklist and teacher observation. The teacher uses a rubric to evaluate each group's written product and their collaborative process.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group posts their digital pamphlet or social media text on a shared platform (e.g., a collaborative document, a class blog, or a projected screen). Groups then briefly present their work to the class or a rotating partner group, explaining their chosen topic and the key information/instructions. Peer groups provide constructive feedback based on a checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Rubric & Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of passive voice to explain support structures (Grammar).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modals for necessity (Grammar).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of present perfect continuous for descriptions (Grammar).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear and relevant use of target vocabulary (Vocabulary).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of instructions/message for the target audience (Writing organization).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of collaborative discussion and problem-solving within the group (Sociolinguistic/Collaboration).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall grammatical accuracy and spelling (Writing mechanics).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List one new thing you learned about writing instructions or social media posts for community outreach."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which grammatical structure (passive voice, modals, or present perfect continuous) did you find most challenging to use correctly in your writing today, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the collaborative writing process did your group find most effective, and what could be improved for next time?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has this lesson changed your perspective on how you can contribute to or communicate about community support?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of student engagement and achieving the writing outcomes? What adjustments are needed for reinforcing grammar points, especially the present perfect continuous for detailed descriptions? Were students able to collaboratively lead discussions about volunteer projects and offer diverse solutions? How can I scaffold the revision process further in future writing tasks?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will involve students summarizing and relaying information about government and community support to different audiences and carrying out a 21st Century Skills Project. The writing skills practiced today, particularly clear instructions and collaborative digital communication, will be crucial for the final project, which may involve creating public service announcements or informational guides.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>